     → INCREASE resource_calendar: The shift timing was chosen to overlap the observed peak-arrival window (8am-8pm, with Mondays and Fridays heaviest); placing additional staff in this window rather than uniformly extending coverage delivered the best waiting-time reduction per added resource. — Evidence: 8am-4pm additional shift outperformed alternative 3 (10am-5pm) and alternative 5 (split shifts) on both cost and waiting-time criteria [papers: [20]]
  4. Reduce emergency department patient waiting time through triage process redesign (domain: healthcare)
     → REASSIGN resource_activity_assignment: Under the current process, doctors handled all level 1-3 patients (over 80% of arrivals) while physician assistants only handled levels 4-5 (under 20%), resulting in 86.4% doctor utilization vs 12.1% physician assistant utilization. Evenly distributing level 3 patients (55% of all arrivals) between doctors and physician assistants balanced workload and reduced the bottleneck at the doctor assessment stage. — Evidence: 20% reduction in average waiting time (89.9 to ~72 min); physician assistant utilization increased from 12.1% to ~35% [papers: [13]]

